WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.916
In the demonstration, several visible places share
the same underlying queue.

00:00:04.916 --> 00:00:08.172
Do not count every green node as a separate server.

00:00:08.172 --> 00:00:14.429
Trace one request and identify its resource
mappings, scheduling, server count and service
demand.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:20.814
Shared queues explain why different request classes
can interfere even when their diagram paths look

00:00:20.814 --> 00:00:21.389
separate.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.152
A capacity change and an asynchronous redesign
answer different questions.

00:00:26.152 --> 00:00:32.188
More servers can reduce queueing; moving work off
the response path can reduce waiting without

00:00:32.188 --> 00:00:33.344
removing the work.

00:00:33.344 --> 00:00:37.004
Compare throughput, utilization and completion
semantics.

00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:42.976
Also distinguish a confidence interval for a
simulation estimate from a percentile of request

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:43.489
latency.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.577
Our small lab illustrates queueing amplification.

00:00:46.577 --> 00:00:52.668
At seventy-five requests per second, changing mean
service from eight to ten milliseconds changes

00:00:52.668 --> 00:00:56.310
predicted mean response from twenty to forty
milliseconds.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.449
This relies on explicit single-server assumptions.

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:05.666
Use it to understand the mechanism, then build and
validate a model appropriate to the real system.
